  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Word order: subject – object – verb",
      "body": "Malayalam puts the **verb at the end** of the sentence. The usual order is Subject – (Time) – (Place) – Object – Verb: 'I tea drink', not 'I drink tea'. There are **no articles** (a/the); ഒരു *oru* 'one' can be used like 'a'.\n\nBecause nouns carry case endings, word order is fairly flexible for emphasis, but the verb almost always stays last. Questions keep the same order — you just add a question word or the question ending -ō.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "ഞാൻ ചായ കുടിക്കുന്നു.", "roman": "ñān chāya kuṭikkunnu.", "en": "I drink / am drinking tea. (I tea drink)" },
        { "native": "അവൻ മലയാളം പഠിക്കുന്നു.", "roman": "avan malayāḷam paṭhikkunnu.", "en": "He is learning Malayalam." },
        { "native": "അമ്മ ചോറ് ഉണ്ടാക്കുന്നു.", "roman": "amma chōṟŭ uṇḍākkunnu.", "en": "Mother is making rice." },
        { "native": "ഞാൻ നാളെ കൊച്ചിയിൽ പോകും.", "roman": "ñān nāḷe kocchiyil pōkum.", "en": "I'll go to Kochi tomorrow. (I tomorrow Kochi-in go-will)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Which 'you'? നീ, നിങ്ങൾ, താങ്കൾ — and titles",
      "body": "**നീ** *nī* is intimate: for close friends, younger siblings, children. Using it to a stranger or an older person is rude.\n\n**നിങ്ങൾ** *niṅṅaḷ* is the plural 'you' and also a polite singular — safe with strangers of your own age or younger, shopkeepers, colleagues. Some elders still feel it is a bit direct.\n\n**താങ്കൾ** *tāṅgaḷ* is formal and respectful (speeches, interviews, letters). In real life, Malayalis very often **avoid the pronoun altogether** and use a title instead: ചേട്ടൻ *chēṭṭan* ('elder brother', for an older man), ചേച്ചി *chēcchi* ('elder sister'), സാർ, മാഡം, or ടീച്ചർ.\n\nThe verb itself doesn't change for 'you' (see unit 2), but imperatives do: informal വാ *vā* 'come', polite വരൂ *varū* 'please come'.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "നീ വാ.", "roman": "nī vā.", "en": "You come. (informal)" },
        { "native": "നിങ്ങൾ വരൂ.", "roman": "niṅṅaḷ varū.", "en": "Please come. (polite)" },
        { "native": "താങ്കൾ ഇരിക്കൂ.", "roman": "tāṅgaḷ irikkū.", "en": "Please be seated. (formal)" },
        { "native": "ചേട്ടൻ എവിടെ പോകുന്നു?", "roman": "chēṭṭan eviṭe pōkunnu?", "en": "Where are you going? (to an older man, lit. 'where is brother going?')" },
        { "native": "സാർ, ഒരു മിനിറ്റ്.", "roman": "sār, oru miniṯṯŭ.", "en": "Sir, one minute." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "dialogue": {
